{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"195c6913\" lines=\"37-52\">\n<description>\nSimulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).\n- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.\n- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.\n\nRule:\n- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.\n- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.\n- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).\n- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.\n\nKey insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"135a2760\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nRepair noisy periodic patterns inside frame-delimited grid regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frame color (most common) forms full rows/cols, dividing the grid into rectangular regions.\n- bg (second most common) fills empty space; each region contains a small colored tile repeated periodically with a few corrupted pixels.\n\nRule:\n- For each region, find the bounding box of non-bg content.\n- Infer the smallest repeating tile that explains the pattern (majority vote per residue position), requiring it repeat at least 2x per axis.\n- Replace the region with the clean tiled version.\n\nKey insight: Tile dimensions are unknown and must be inferred; prefer the smallest tile with genuine repetition over one that memorizes the entire region.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a32d8b75\" lines=\"262-275\">\n<description>\nAssemble and place enlarged sprites on a canvas using legend-panel instructions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color-6 separators divide the grid into a left legend column and a main canvas.\n- Each legend panel has (top to bottom): a unit sprite, a frame template, a 5x5 bordered square with a position-marker pixel, and a 3x3 rotation clock-hand.\n\nRule:\n- Tile the unit sprite into each active cell of the frame to build an enlarged composite.\n- Rotate the composite per the clock-hand direction (up=0, right=90, down=180, left=270 degrees).\n- Place it on the canvas anchored at the position encoded by the marker pixel's row/col in the bordered square's 3x3 inner grid.\n\nKey insight: The marker pixel's position in a 3x3 grid jointly encodes vertical and horizontal anchor alignment (start/middle/end).\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7ed72f31\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.\n- Colored sprites appear near red markers.\n\nRule:\n- Red line within a sprite (vertical or horizontal) → mirror payload across that axis.\n- Single red pixel in a mixed sprite → point-reflect payload around it.\n- Isolated red pixel (alone, no payload) → point-reflect each adjacent 8-connected color component.\n- Originals remain; mirrored pixels overlay.\n\nKey insight: Red marker geometry (line vs single pixel) determines reflection type; isolated vs embedded red determines what gets reflected.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\nRule:\n- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.\n- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.\n- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.\n\nKey insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n<description>\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"65b59efc\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDecode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into K tile columns (each N wide) separated by 1-col dashed separators.\n- Top band (rows 0..N-1): K template masks, one per tile column.\n- Middle band (rows N+1..2N): marker pixels whose color picks a template.\n- Bottom band (rows 2N+2..): K palette swatches giving the output color per template.\n- Separator rows at rows N and 2N+1 (dashed pattern of sep color).\n\nRule:\n- Each marker's local position (lr, lc) within its tile column determines which N×N output block to stamp.\n- The marker's color identifies which template to use; the palette recolors the template.\n\nKey insight: Marker color encodes template index; local position within tile column encodes stamp block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"aa4ec2a5\" lines=\"52-66\">\n<description>\nAdd red outlines around objects, and mark enclosed holes inside hollow objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly uniform background contains one or more 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Some objects may enclose 4-connected background holes inside their bbox.\n\nRule:\n- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).\n- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).\n- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.\n\nKey insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects\nthat actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\n\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
